{"causes": ["The ongoing succession crisis among the sons of Sultan Bayezid II created a volatile political environment demanding experienced leadership.", "Sultan Bayezid II's advanced age and declining health necessitated a strong Grand Vizier to manage the empire's day-to-day affairs and internal factions.", "The strategic importance of Anatolia and the Balkans meant that non-devşirme officials like Koca Mustafa Paşa often gained influence through regional administrative experience."], "effects": ["Koca Mustafa Paşa's execution solidified Sultan Selim I's control over the Ottoman bureaucracy by eliminating a high-ranking official associated with the previous regime.", "His brief tenure and rapid downfall highlighted the inherent dangers and precariousness of high office during periods of intense dynastic struggle within the Ottoman Empire.", "The purge of officials like Koca Mustafa Paşa by Selim I contributed to the consolidation of a more centralized and loyal administration under the new sultan."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "bayezid-ii", "sourceName": "Bayezid II", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "koca-mustafa-paa", "targetName": "Koca Mustafa Paşa", "context": "Koca Mustafa Paşa served as Grand Vizier under Sultan Bayezid II from 1511 until Bayezid's deposition in April 1512, attempting to manage the empire's affairs."}, {"sourceSlug": "koca-mustafa-paa", "sourceName": "Koca Mustafa Paşa", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "ottoman-succession-crisis-1511-1512", "targetName": "Ottoman Succession Crisis (1511-1512)", "context": "Koca Mustafa Paşa was a central figure in the Ottoman court during the intense power struggle between Bayezid II's sons, Şehzade Ahmed and Şehzade Selim, from 1511 to 1512."}, {"sourceSlug": "selim-i", "sourceName": "Selim I", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "koca-mustafa-paa", "targetName": "Koca Mustafa Paşa", "context": "Sultan Selim I ordered the execution of Koca Mustafa Paşa on November 23, 1512, shortly after consolidating his power, to eliminate potential rivals."}, {"sourceSlug": "koca-mustafa-paa", "sourceName": "Koca Mustafa Paşa", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "istanbul-ottoman-empire", "targetName": "Istanbul, Ottoman Empire", "context": "Koca Mustafa Paşa served as Grand Vizier from the imperial capital of Istanbul, where he was also executed in 1512."}, {"sourceSlug": "ottoman-empire-early-modern", "sourceName": "Ottoman Empire (Early Modern)", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "koca-mustafa-paa", "targetName": "Koca Mustafa Paşa", "context": "Koca Mustafa Paşa was a high-ranking statesman within the vast administrative structure of the Early Modern Ottoman Empire."}], "places": [{"name": "Istanbul, Ottoman Empire", "role": "Seat of Power"}, {"name": "Bursa, Ottoman Empire", "role": "Execution Site"}, {"name": "Anatolia, Ottoman Empire", "role": "Region of Origin/Influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Koca Mustafa Paşa", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "koca-mustafa-paşa"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6424688", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koca_Mustafa_Pasha"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Koca Mustafa Paşa left a notable mark on Medieval history, documented across 0 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped political institutions and governance practices; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati. Their legacy is studied through the lens of political thought.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Koca Mustafa Paşa's brief but pivotal tenure as Grand Vizier and his subsequent execution exemplify the ruthless consolidation of power by Selim I, directly influencing the character of the Ottoman administration for decades to come. His story highlights the perilous nature of high office during a critical dynastic transition.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-16T12:10:22Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31946220649", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Koca Mustafa Pasha was an Ottoman statesman. He was grand vizier of the Ottoman Empire from 1511 to 1512. He was Roman (Rum) and probably not a devşirme.", "newValue": "Koca Mustafa Paşa, an Ottoman statesman of obscure origins, rose to the pinnacle of imperial power as Grand Vizier from 1511 until his execution on November 23, 1512. Identified as a 'Rum' (Roman), implying a native Anatolian or Balkan Greek background rather than a product of the devşirme system, his brief tenure coincided with a period of significant political intrigue and succession anxieties within the Ottoman Empire. His appointment under Sultan Bayezid II placed him at the heart of the Ottoman administration during the tumultuous final years of the aging sultan's reign, a period marked by the rise of his ambitious son, Selim I.

During his short but impactful vizierate, Koca Mustafa Paşa navigated the complex power struggles between Bayezid II and his sons, particularly the fierce rivalry between Şehzade Ahmed and Şehzade Selim for the throne. He was initially a key supporter of Bayezid II, attempting to stabilize the empire amidst internal revolts and external pressures. His administrative efforts aimed at maintaining order and ensuring the smooth functioning of the state apparatus, even as the Ottoman court became increasingly polarized by the succession crisis. His actions, however, ultimately failed to prevent the eventual deposition of Bayezid II and the ascension of Selim I, a pivotal moment that reshaped the empire's trajectory.

The abrupt end of Koca Mustafa Paşa's career, marked by his execution by the newly enthroned Selim I, underscores the brutal realities of Ottoman court politics during a dynastic transition. His downfall served as a stark warning to other officials and solidified Selim I's grip on power, eliminating potential rivals or those perceived as loyal to the old regime. While his direct legacy was truncated, his fate exemplifies the perilous nature of high office and the relentless consolidation of authority that characterized Selim I's reign, paving the way for the latter's aggressive expansionist policies."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-16T12:10:22Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31946220649", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1500"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1500"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing succession crisis among the sons of Sultan Bayezid II created a volatile political environment demanding experienced leadership.", "Sultan Bayezid II's advanced age and declining health necessitated a strong Grand Vizier to manage the empire's day-to-day affairs and internal factions.", "The strategic importance of Anatolia and the Balkans meant that non-devşirme officials like Koca Mustafa Paşa often gained influence through regional administrative experience."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-16T12:10:22Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31946220649", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1512-11-23"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1512-11-23"}], "newValue": ["Koca Mustafa Paşa's execution solidified Sultan Selim I's control over the Ottoman bureau